@@ -7,6 +7,9 @@
 ## Pipeline
 
 * `data/manifest.csv` lists decoded ranges: `name,start,end,format,file,description`.
+  Optional `owner`, `selector` and `dispatchers` columns describe reviewed uses
+  for browsing; dispatchers are space-separated 8-digit code addresses. This
+  metadata does not change bytes or turn adjacent labels into logical bounds.
 * `tools/datafmt/<format>.py` decodes a range to a source file (`decode`) and
   encodes the file back to items (`encode`): sized integers, and ROM pointers
   that `tools/symbolize.py` writes as symbols, so decoded tables stay movable.
@@ -56,7 +59,7 @@
 | Format | Range | File | Notes |
 |---|---|---|---|
 | `keyed_callbacks` | `08603300`–`08604998` | `data/actor_registry.csv` | 722 sorted `(key, callback)` records and a zero sentinel. The script `chara` command binary-searches it (`08225844`) and calls the callback. |
-| `callback_array` | Eleven ranges listed in `data/manifest.csv` | `data/actor_commands/*.csv` | Ordered Thumb callback pointers for the common actor commands and ten monster extensions. The command packet's first halfword selects an entry; every non-null pointer remains a linker relocation. |
+| `callback_array` | Ranges listed in `data/manifest.csv` | `data/actor_commands/*.csv`, `data/player_callbacks/*.csv` | Ordered Thumb callback pointers. Actor commands use the packet's first halfword; Player arrays use the documented mode or element selector. Every non-null pointer remains a linker relocation. |
 | `resource_archive` | 10 ranges `08614DCC`–`08D5A85C` | `build/res/LABEL/index.txt` + `HASH.bin` (extracted) | The archives found by `ScriptProc_Start` (anm, map, ptn, fnt, sprites...). Header, sorted hash table and offsets are regenerated; entries are emitted as `.incbin` of their files and may change size. Their contents are position-independent: about 7,300 pointer-like words inside them (tile and map data) are no longer relocated. Verified in-game: growing the first `anm` entry by 37 bytes shifts every archive and leaves all menu and field screenshots identical. |
 | `text_bank` | `08D6B1E4`–`08DD6B96` | `build/text/bank.tsv` (extracted) | Script text, resizable. The gcx section words, 9,977 entry offsets, then strings (`T`, decoded with `data/charmap.tsv`) and binary blobs (`B`, 82 tables read by script code). Verified in-game: a lengthened config help line renders and the field still plays. |
 
@@ -149,3 +152,32 @@
 Tables named as literals in the callback or loader; initialization may
 install another table later. Studio's ROM view gives each table its own block
 and marks exactly 336 bytes across the eleven table extents as decoded.
+
+### Player callback tables
+
+Seven decoded arrays under `data/player_callbacks/` contain 186 entries (744
+bytes). These are fixed physical extents, with the selector and reviewed
+consumer recorded in the manifest:
+
+| Range (end exclusive) | Entries | Selector | Consumer |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| `08610964`–`08610980` | 7 | `actionMode` | `0813FC38` |
+| `08610B70`–`08610C0C` | 39 | `tetherMode` | `0815788C` |
+| `08610C0C`–`08610CA8` | 39 | `tetherMode` | `08157CFC` |
+| `08610CA8`–`08610D18` | 28 | `Player_SelectInteractionMode` result | `0815849C` |
+| `08610D18`–`08610D88` | 28 | `Player_SelectAlternateInteractionMode` result | `08158754` |
+| `08610D88`–`08610E24` | 39 | `tetherMode` | `08158848` |
+| `08610E2C`–`08610E44` | 6 | `element` | `0816233C` |
+
+Studio's **Callback tables** view (`/actors#callbacks`) includes these arrays
+and the actor command tables. It shows ownership, selector, dispatcher links
+and every handler in numeric order. Code pages link exact table-start literals
+back to that view, and the ROM view marks only each decoded extent. The older
+`/actors#commands` links retain the actor-command subset. After editing a CSV,
+manifest metadata or function symbols, reload the page: the server refreshes
+the table index and its dependent actor/name caches.
+
+The gap `08610E24`–`08610E2C` remains undecoded here. The high-ROM region
+`08E87A10`–`08E87B78` also remains outside the manifest: dispatch indices cross
+its apparent subtable labels, so those labels alone do not establish separate
+array bounds. No null entry or unknown extent is silently removed.
